From 4f210c6f8bd638dc4ca6007d43addf26444c7392 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 11:55:36 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(claude_code_ide): serve the Claude Code IDE protocol on
 a loopback WebSocket

Creates crates/claude_code_ide/ and wires it into crates/project and crates/zed,
so the `claude` CLI running in Zed's integrated terminal connects to the editor
the way it does to VS Code and JetBrains: `/ide` finds a server, selections and
diagnostics flow, and a proposed change opens as a real diff tab with Keep and
Reject rather than as a wall of text in the terminal.

Shape. One server per workspace window, bound to 127.0.0.1:0. Each writes
~/.claude/ide/<port>.lock (0600 in a 0700 directory, atomically, so the CLI never
reads a half-written file) carrying a per-window random token, and the terminal
gets CLAUDE_CODE_SSE_PORT and ENABLE_IDE_INTEGRATION so the CLI auto-connects.
The trust boundary is processes running as this user, which is what the official
editor integrations assume too.

`openDiff` blocks until the user decides and then returns the accepted contents;
it deliberately does not write the file, because the CLI performs that write and
doing both would race.

The protocol this speaks is defined by the CLI and documented nowhere, so two
clauses are worth naming here -- both were wrong in this patch's first version,
and both fail silently:

  - The lock file must carry `transport: "ws"`. The CLI parses
    `transport === "ws"` and passes the result on as `useWebSocket`, the key of
    its parser's OUTPUT, which is what the URL branch tests; without `transport`
    it builds http://host:port/sse and reaches nothing. The `useWebSocket: true`
    this lock also writes is not read by the CLI (VS Code's lock has none) and
    stays only as harmless parity with the first fix; serde's camelCase would
    render it `useWebsocket`, hence the explicit #[serde(rename)]. Corrected
    2026-11-03 (story 066): this note said the opposite, against CLI 2.1.251.
  - The handshake must echo `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: mcp`. The CLI offers it on
    every connection and its WebSocket client closes with 1002, "Missing client
    protocol", when the 101 does not name it back.

Neither breaks the build, so neither is caught by anything that checks whether
this patch still applies. zed-patches/scripts/check-protocol.sh is what watches
them, and terminal-ide/CLAUDE.md records how to re-derive them from the shipped
CLI when it next moves.

Upstream PR #58300 was closed unmerged on 2026-06-30. Discussion #58338 is where
this work lives now, and where protocol breakage is reported first.

A server is published -- lock file written AND port handed to the project's
terminals -- only while its workspace has folders (2026-10-01). Every window
opens with a workspace of its own and the projects of its sidebar arrive as
further workspaces, so the first one never gets a folder: on that day 7 windows
held 7 folderless lock files, matching no working directory, alongside two 401
handshakes. Lock and port move together because the CLI reads the token from
the lock file of the port it was given: a port without a lock file would make a
CLI started in that terminal fail the handshake rather than run without an IDE.

`ideName` -- what the CLI's /ide prints as the editor it is connected to -- is
the release channel's display_name(), read when the lock file is written, not a
"Zed" constant (2026-10-07). Under Zeo it prints "Zeo"; on an upstream build it
still prints "Zed", or "Zed Nightly". The CLI only displays the field, so the
value is free to follow the window title. serverInfo.name stays "zed": that one
is a protocol identity, not a label.

Tests added 2026-10-09 (0.2.2_p20261009), each verified to fail with the guarded line reverted:
the lock-file module takes its directory as a parameter (`create_in`, `remove_in`,
`lock_dir_from`), so a test can check the real file -- the directory ends 0700
even when it existed wider, the file 0600, no temp file left, `remove` twice is
fine, and CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR's set/empty/unset rule. The wire-format test now reads
the file `create` wrote instead of a struct it built, and the tautological
`lock_dir_honors_claude_config_dir` asserts the rule for real. The handshake
rejects a wrong token, a token with a suffix, a prefix of it, a case-folded copy
and an empty one (401), and still upgrades the exact token: a relaxed comparison
would have passed the old missing-header test. `lock_path` went unused and was
removed.

More tests (0.2.3_p20261009): the folderless-window withdrawal (`advertise`, split from
`publish` so it needs no Project), the frame loop past the 101 (a text request gets
its response, a ping its pong), JSON-RPC other than 2.0 and a tools/call with no
name are invalid. The terminal environment is one helper,
`insert_claude_code_ide_env`, called by all three terminal paths and tested.

Protocol alignment with the VS Code extension (story 066, 2026-11-03, CLI 2.1.316):
- `openDiff` answers `TAB_CLOSED` when the tab is closed without Keep or Reject,
  and when its sender is dropped; only Reject answers `DIFF_REJECTED`. The CLI
  then keeps its proposed edit unsaved instead of recording a rejection.
- `selection_changed` is pushed: at most one per 100 ms per connection, the last
  state winning, and the current selection replayed 500 ms after a CLI connects,
  as VS Code does. Positions are 0-based (the CLI adds 1). A terminal or panel
  taking focus keeps the last selection; an editor with no file -- or nothing
  open -- clears it with a zero-width 0:0 selection, because the CLI ignores a
  null one (`if(!Be?.start||!Be?.end)return;`). `getCurrentSelection` and
  `getLatestSelection` answer from the same state.
- "Mention Selection in Claude Code" (`claude_code_ide::MentionSelectionInClaudeCode`,
  editor context menu, palette, ctrl-alt-shift-m / cmd-alt-shift-m -- no default
  binding used that chord) sends `at_mentioned` with 0-based lines; with no CLI
  connected it says "No Claude Code session is connected to this workspace".
  Hidden with the rest of the AI actions under `disable_ai`.
- Requests now run concurrently with the frame loop: an undecided `openDiff` used
  to hold back every push and ping on its connection.
Proven live: a real `claude` 2.1.316 on this build showed "Selected 3 lines from
sample.rs" and turned the mention into `@sample.rs#L1-3`; on the shipped build it
received neither notification. The crate is now rustfmt-clean.
---

